sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/aristotle-ga03-12> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "In some cartilaginous fishes the egg stays attached to the uterus, and when the young fish is complete it is still connected to the uterus by an umbilical cord after the yolk is used up, as in the smooth dogfish.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Live-bearing selachians keep the young attached to the uterus by an umbilicus";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Aristotle, On the Generation of Animals (De Generatione Animalium), Book III; translated by Arthur Platt, The Works of Aristotle Translated into English, ed. J. A. Smith and W. D. Ross, Oxford: Clarendon Press, vol. V (1912). Scope: some selachians, notably the 'smooth hound' (Mustelus, smooth dogfish)"
}
